+# Software Architecture and Design Specification (SAD) — CampusPool
+
+**Project:** CampusPool
+**Version:** 0.1 (proposed draft)
+**Date:** 26 October 2026
+**Status:** DRAFT — not submitted, approved, or signed
+**Related documents:** [SRS_DRAFT.md](SRS_DRAFT.md), [TEST_PLAN_DRAFT.md](TEST_PLAN_DRAFT.md), [RTM_DRAFT.md](RTM_DRAFT.md)
+
+## Revision history and approvals
+
+This is a new AI-assisted proposed document based on the current prototype and course templates. No author, reviewer, signature or approval is asserted.
+
+## 1. Introduction
+
+### 1.1 Purpose
+
+Describe the current Sprint 1 architecture and the design needed to verify ride publication, matching, booking, cancellation and synthetic wallet behavior.
+
+### 1.2 Scope
+
+This SAD describes the Laravel/MySQL/browser prototype and its isolated Docker verification. Next.js, NoSQL/vector retrieval, live routing, production identity and real payment architecture remain future work.
+
+### 1.3 Audience
+
+Students, instructors, developers, testers and maintainers.
+
+### 1.4 Definitions
+
+API: application programming interface. DTO: data transfer object. RTM: requirements traceability matrix. STP: software test plan. Quote: signed search result.
+
+## 2. Document overview
+
+The document presents the architecture, component responsibilities, data boundaries, API design, security controls, sequences, risks and open work.
+
+## 3. Architecture
+
+### 3.1 Goals and constraints
+
+Goals are deterministic verification, transactional booking state, explicit authorization and understandable UI evidence. Constraints are the inherited Laravel/MySQL baseline, synthetic route provider, incomplete course documents and no production credentials.
+
+### 3.2 Stakeholders and concerns
+
+Riders need clear availability and status. Drivers need safe publication and acceptance. Instructors need reproducible evidence and traceability. Developers need isolated migrations, tests and attribution. Reviewers need clear limits on synthetic behavior.
+
+### 3.3 Component diagram
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TB
+  Browser[Browser commute UI\nHTML/CSS/JS] -->|JSON + bearer session| API[Laravel Commute API]
+  API --> Auth[Authorization and demo-session policy]
+  API --> Match[Matching and quote service]
+  API --> Life[Booking lifecycle service]
+  API --> Wallet[Demo wallet/refund service]
+  Auth --> SQL[(MySQL transactional schema)]
+  Match --> SQL
+  Life --> SQL
+  Wallet --> SQL
+  Match --> Route[Routing adapter\nsynthetic fixture in CI]
+  CI[GitHub Actions / Docker Compose] --> Verify[Package, syntax, migration and smoke verification]
+  Verify --> API
+```
+
+### 3.4 Component descriptions
+
+| Component | Responsibility | Current source |
+|---|---|---|
+| Browser commute UI | Profile, offer, search, booking and cancellation presentation | `prototype/campuspool-extension/web/` |
+| Commute API | Request validation, response envelope and route binding | `prototype/campuspool-extension/routes/commute.php`, `Mobility/Commute/CommuteController.php` |
+| Matching/quote service | Time, direction, vehicle, capacity and route-term filtering | `Mobility/Commute/CommuteMatchingService.php` |
+| Lifecycle service | Request, accept/reject/cancel, booking DTO and transactions | `Mobility/Commute/CommuteService.php` |
+| MySQL schema | Users, trips, passengers, lifecycle, wallet and constraints | `prototype/database/migrations/`, extension migrations |
+| Verification runner | Fresh package, syntax, migration, HTTP health and smoke journey | `prototype/reproduction/verify-package.sh`, `smoke-booking.py` |
+
+### 3.5 Architecture pattern and rationale
+
+The current design is a layered modular monolith: browser, HTTP/controller, domain services and relational persistence. This fits Sprint 1 review and keeps booking authority in one transactional database. A distributed microservice split would add deployment and consistency risk without a demonstrated requirement.
+
+### 3.6 Technology stack and data stores
+
+Laravel/PHP, MySQL 8, browser JavaScript, Python verification and Docker Compose. The DBMS course requirement for a second NoSQL/vector database and Next.js interface is not satisfied by this current Sprint 1 baseline and is recorded in [COURSE_REQUIREMENTS_RECONCILIATION.md](COURSE_REQUIREMENTS_RECONCILIATION.md).
+
+### 3.7 Risks and mitigations
+
+Synthetic routing can hide provider failures; CI and the runbook label it. Shared service edits can conflict; issue packages apply sequentially. Missing submitted documents can cause traceability errors; all IDs in this package are marked proposed. Real payments and identity are excluded from fixtures.
+
+### 3.8 Traceability to requirements
+
+`CP-F-009..020` map to matching/lifecycle services; `CP-NF-002` maps to MySQL transactions; `CP-SEC-001..005` map to policy, route validation and redacted verification. Full provisional mapping is in [RTM_DRAFT.md](RTM_DRAFT.md).
+
+### 3.9 Security architecture
+
+Authentication is represented by the current API session mechanism. Authorization is enforced in controller/service paths for driver, rider and owner actions. Quote and idempotency tokens are request-bound. Logs and CI output must redact tokens and identity/licence data. Production threat modelling, TLS termination, secret rotation and identity-provider review remain open.
+
+## 4. Design
+
+### 4.1 Design overview
+
+The API validates input at the boundary, delegates matching or lifecycle transitions to domain services, and commits state changes in MySQL transactions. Search returns a quote token; request consumes that quote; acceptance locks the trip and reserves capacity; cancellation records state and applies one refund effect.
+
+### 4.2 UML sequence diagrams
+
+#### Search and request
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+  actor Rider
+  participant UI as Browser UI
+  participant API as Commute API
+  participant Match as Matching service
+  participant DB as MySQL
+  Rider->>UI: Select date, direction, vehicle and time
+  UI->>API: GET /rides with filters
+  API->>Match: Validate filters and inspect offers
+  Match->>DB: Read future published rides and capacity
+  DB-->>Match: Matching ride rows
+  Match-->>API: Ride DTO + quote token
+  API-->>UI: Search results
+  Rider->>UI: Request seat
+  UI->>API: POST /rides/{trip}/request quote_token
+  API->>Match: Verify quote binding and freshness
+  Match->>DB: Insert pending booking transactionally
+  DB-->>API: Booking id/state=pending
+  API-->>UI: Pending booking
+```
+
+#### Accept, pay, cancel and refund
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+  actor Driver
+  actor Rider
+  participant UI as Browser UI
+  participant API as Commute API
+  participant Life as Lifecycle service
+  participant DB as MySQL
+  Driver->>UI: Accept pending request
+  UI->>API: POST /bookings/{id}/accept
+  API->>Life: Authorize owner and lock trip/booking
+  Life->>DB: Check free seat and set confirmed
+  DB-->>Life: Confirmed state
+  Life-->>UI: Confirmed booking
+  Rider->>UI: Top up and pay
+  UI->>API: POST /bookings/{id}/pay
+  API->>DB: Lock wallet, create one hold and ledger entry
+  DB-->>UI: wallet_state=held
+  Rider->>UI: Cancel booking
+  UI->>API: POST /bookings/{id}/cancel
+  API->>Life: Lock booking and apply cancellation
+  Life->>DB: Set cancelled, restore capacity, refund held amount once
+  DB-->>UI: Cancelled history and wallet balance
+```
+
+### 4.3 API design
+
+**Component 1 — Matching API**
+
+`GET /api/mobility/commute/rides?direction=outbound&date=YYYY-MM-DD&start_time=08:00&end_time=09:00&vehicle_type=car` returns `data[]` ride DTOs with `trip_id`, vehicle, departure, seats and `quote_token`. Invalid ranges return validation errors; stale or changed quotes are rejected when requesting.
+
+**Component 2 — Booking/lifecycle API**
+
+`POST /api/mobility/commute/bookings/{booking}/accept|reject|cancel` requires the authenticated actor and returns `data.request_state`. Acceptance is state `1`, rejection is state `2` and cancellation is state `3` in the current prototype. Unauthorized actors, unavailable capacity and changed state return controlled errors.
+
+### 4.4 Error handling, logging and monitoring
+
+Validation and domain errors use structured JSON. Verification scripts report status and safe error codes only. Tokens, credentials, licence data and private identity values must not be printed. Production metrics, alerting and log retention are open.
+
+### 4.5 UX design
+
+Forms should show vehicle/date/capacity errors near their fields; search should preserve pickup/fare summaries when route rendering fails; pending, confirmed and cancelled states must remain visible. Accessibility and real device testing require manual follow-up.
+
+### 4.6 Open issues and next steps
+
+Complete the faculty-approved SRS/STP/SAD if available, add Next.js and the required second data paradigm for DBMS, obtain instructor-approved two-person scope, perform originality review, configure real provider profiles separately and record the actual video. Waitlist/rematching remains Sprint 2.
+
+## 5. Appendices
+
+References are the saved faculty SRS/SAD/STP templates, the 6 October SE Part-2 handout, the 9 October DBMS requirements and the current official repository baseline. No template is represented as an approved CampusPool submission.
